    "['switchport trunk native vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"switchport trunk native vlan 10\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10\\n##\\n\\nThis command:\\n1. Selects the interface (ge-1/0/1 equivalent to GigabitEthernet1/0/1)\\n2. Specifies the Ethernet switching family\\n3. Sets VLAN 10 as the native VLAN for the trunk\\n\\nNote that in Juniper:\\n- Interface names use a different format (ge-x/y/z instead of GigabitEthernet)\\n- You need to specify the unit (typically 0) and family (ethernet-switching)\\n- The concept is the same, setting the untagged VLAN for trunk ports', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco command `switchport trunk allowed vlan 20`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The interface naming convention changes from `GigabitEthernet1/0/1` to `ge-1/0/1` in Juniper.\\n2. The command puts VLAN 20 as the only allowed VLAN on the trunk interface (equivalent to the Cisco command).\\n3. You would need to adjust the interface name (`ge-1/0/1`) to match the specific interface you're configuring.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk native vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper, the equivalent command sets the native VLAN ID for trunking on an Ethernet-switching interface. Note that Juniper uses a different syntax structure where you specify the interface, unit, and protocol family before setting the VLAN parameters.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The interface naming convention changes from `GigabitEthernet1/0/1` to `ge-1/0/1` in Juniper\\n2. Juniper requires specifying the unit (typically 0) and family type\\n3. In Juniper, you don\\'t need separate \"allowed VLAN\" commands - you just specify the VLAN members directly\\n4. The command assumes the VLANs are already defined in the Juniper configuration (equivalent to the `vlan 10,20` statement in Cisco)', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20'}]]": 1,
